#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444620} :activity/id #uuid "0bdff687-f8d4-5855-9373-b5ab9e4534b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188 :topic/id #uuid "34eb87b9-7036-55c0-88eb-1b1efbd11584" :topic/math-academy-id 3178} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f5f2fd-94a6-51c3-ac48-99d59a93e907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444605} {:answer/id #uuid "8524a039-125d-5c28-8aaa-2b417ce25d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444606} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cc803d-28d2-5c8a-ad4d-e88ee6c149ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444608} {:answer/id #uuid "ad810e29-dc47-543e-aeb1-8297ce882407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444610} {:answer/id #uuid "e9761621-383d-53ec-8615-e110f65c1ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444610} :answer-field/id #uuid "63f86b8b-b32a-5de1-8bfa-b93d1b01899d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "44a48f57-f74b-5294-bb24-4cee5d89d1d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161149" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2z,\\,x+y,\\,y-z⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2z,\\,x+y,\\,y-z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨2z,\\,x+y,\\,y-z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(2z-(x+y)+(y-z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z-x}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{z-x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{∬}_{S}z-x\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "03b4b191-1c32-5328-8c44-46701817acdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23676b92-59ce-549e-bb44-0ce2c22aff60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444601} {:answer/id #uuid "30a14e60-74d3-59a0-9d7c-8bfb0dc4a3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444603} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7915a4-09c6-54d8-8b86-d3abcf6997b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444607} {:answer/id #uuid "ca06b212-b30b-58a7-bceb-f7e5db8c00f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444612} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e593f7-c2c1-5fc5-8a3d-c699694f0c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444614} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac4cbf3-d6da-5e60-8eb4-cf067330135d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ae7b222-1017-5e06-9f85-8323117b3184" :question/math-academy-id "q-162158" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z+5,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x+2y+1$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{2}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z+5,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+6,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}((x+2y+6)+2(x-2y)-(3x-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(x+2y+6+2x-4y-3x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0ad1dc8f-1c73-5500-a47a-c0fda9ab766e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2720bb2d-ef3c-5bd3-b5d1-47e2a36e5b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy+yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275834} {:answer/id #uuid "30f13b93-277e-5c15-b9c8-932581f5f992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy-2yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275912} {:answer/id #uuid "3b883b06-8332-53dc-bb7f-68fb90e59ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3xz-yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275997} {:answer/id #uuid "74a4ce69-d793-5db1-9424-5b3b421ad0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276532} {:answer/id #uuid "8f857f74-c7bf-534b-90d2-dc5735bc9d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy-yz+2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276777} :answer-field/id #uuid "74b1826a-ee9f-5272-9977-4058c1ea92ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe4ccf73-c8f1-5e87-8e15-f27def60e911" :question/math-academy-id "q-161200" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(xy-yz+2xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}xy-yz+2xz\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d884d62-3c79-5671-b709-2a062cef2457" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444621}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7ca8ec-891f-5ccc-985b-4e4a85531ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444602} {:answer/id #uuid "3ccaaf5e-0e10-5c99-a0bc-d8a834cb86f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444604} {:answer/id #uuid "a5039a5b-f9d4-5a06-b32c-4be440d0e059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444609} {:answer/id #uuid "c73e0516-826a-57fd-99ad-0e6ddc957f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444611} {:answer/id #uuid "f817a54c-d2b8-5a8f-9b28-dbefeb579916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444611} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6a2aa9-7d14-5b93-966f-5ef76762e118" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "627a222e-9363-5b8b-b1ec-0730c89cfcbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-161193" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{3}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+y,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+y)-y+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}}y\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}-2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b60e2d93-3137-5009-9f82-8e66614f6b08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419c0982-db99-5289-b52f-a761ea3d1bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y-x-2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401221} {:answer/id #uuid "d412c461-4939-5aa5-9ddd-df78a8e85bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401222} {:answer/id #uuid "a93a034f-199c-5c52-af3b-9d0a37596a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401223} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbb580a-2ddc-5e43-a8f4-c46a6440cff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401224} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a49a16-7b19-549f-8e35-18199cc77301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401225}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401224} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21eed7a-f07f-5d01-b24f-3b6ba15f83ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd68aee4-128c-55a1-8849-b847e40933c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161198" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{z}^{2}-x+2(y-{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y-x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2y-x}{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}2y-x\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de780f69-4606-5409-93bc-2ffc77e5cb9b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444622}}] :activity/title "Flux in Three-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444600}]}
